claptimes5 (2023-05-22T15:42:24Z): I just had the same problem, without changing anything. For me, this was because my server's burst capacity as exhausted. I migrated my blog to a larger server and slow queries resolved.  | brvnnghi (2023-05-23T01:17:01Z): > I just had the same problem, without changing anything. For me, this was because my server's burst capacity as exhausted. I migrated my blog to a larger server and slow queries resolved.

May I know your server specs for such 100,000 posts without lagging?

I have not approached that number, but [my photo site](https://www.hires.vn/) will soon reach that. Currently running on a Ghost(Pro) subscription, but I am going to migrate to a VPS instance for better database control.

Thanks. | alexandrucancescu (2023-05-23T15:38:08Z): I am using a dedicated database with 3 vCPUs, 4 GB RAM and a VPS with 2 ARM Cores 64bit and 4GB of RAM. I will be moving to a 16GB RAM and 4 AMD Epyc cores this week, but I do not have much hope for great improvements. | cathysarisky (2023-05-24T13:33:05Z): Hi!  This discussion might be better on the forums, since it's not clear this is a Ghost core bug.  It might be server specs.  Could I encourage you to try the Casper theme temporarily?  Tripoli has some extra #get requests on the front page that may be the source of the problem - it'd be good to see if the problem is isolated to that theme or whether it's more general.

(100k posts is a lot!  Wow!) | alexandrucancescu (2023-05-24T13:38:25Z): @cathysarisky I did try Casper, it also calls the slow queries. I also tried 3 server specs, now I switched to a 16GB RAM, 4 dedicated AMD Epyc Cores, and for now only Ghost and mysql run on it. Even with this setup it still takes over 5 seconds for the front page to load. 

In my opinion it is a bug, the front page breaks because I have a lot of articles, while there is no mention in any Ghost documentation that Ghost is not supposed to be used with such a large database.

If someone from the Ghost dev teams reads this, is there any way to allow more time to the helpers to finish? This way I can setup a cache and ensure that I don't cache a broken page. | github-actions (2023-06-15T19:05:25Z): This issue is currently awaiting triage from @daniellockyer. We're having a busy time right now, but we'll update this issue ASAP. If you have any more information to help us triage faster please leave us some comments. Thank you for understanding 🙂 | daniellockyer (2023-06-20T17:31:27Z): Hey @alexandrucancescu - thanks for the detailed bug report. We generally spec the performance of Ghost against a fewer number of posts, but we're constantly re-evaluating this given the size of sites we're starting to see. Performance is constantly on our radar but I can't give any guarantees as to when this specific issue will be improved | alexandrucancescu (2023-06-20T17:34:01Z): Hi @daniellockyer! Thanks for your response. What would help most in my situation is a way to increase the time allowed for the helpers to complete their queries. That way I can be certain that the cached pages are not broken. | daniellockyer (2023-06-20T20:00:19Z): @alexandrucancescu In that case, I can help you! You can add the following to your `config.production.json`:

```json
    "optimization": {
        "getHelper": {
            "timeout": {
                "threshold": 10000
            }
        }
    },
```

or replace 10000 with whatever is appropriate for you.

and then restart Ghost 🙂  | alexandrucancescu (2023-06-20T20:01:21Z): Thanks! | github-actions (2023-10-18T20:04:40Z): Our bot has automatically marked this issue as stale because there has not been any activity here in some time.

